### What is backpressure?

Backpressure is the mechanism by which a slow consumer signals that it cannot keep up, so the system avoids overwhelming it. In push-based systems, this requires explicit flow control. In Kafka, the pull model provides it automatically.

```java
package com.bank.backpressure;

public class PushVsPull {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Push-based (e.g., RabbitMQ default):");
        System.out.println("  Broker pushes messages to consumer");
        System.out.println("  Slow consumer -> memory overflow / drop");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Pull-based (Kafka):");
        System.out.println("  Consumer calls poll() at own pace");
        System.out.println("  Slow consumer -> polls less -> lag grows");
        System.out.println("  Nothing lost, nothing overflows");
    }
}
```

```text
Push-based (e.g., RabbitMQ default):
  Broker pushes messages to consumer
  Slow consumer -> memory overflow / drop

Pull-based (Kafka):
  Consumer calls poll() at own pace
  Slow consumer -> polls less -> lag grows
  Nothing lost, nothing overflows
```

### How does the pull model handle backpressure?

Consumers call `poll()` to request records. The broker never forces records onto a consumer. A busy consumer simply does not poll as often or fetches fewer records per poll. The records it has not fetched stay in the partition log, which is durable and bounded only by retention.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecords;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class PullModelDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "broker1:9092");
        props.put("group.id", "settlement-service");
        props.put("max.poll.records", "100");
        props.put("enable.auto.commit", "false");
        props.put("key.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        props.put("value.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");

        try (KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props)) {
            consumer.subscribe(Collections.singletonList("transfers"));
            while (true) {
                // Consumer controls intake rate via poll()
                ConsumerRecords<String, String> records =
                    consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
                // Process at own pace
                records.forEach(r -> processTransfer(r.value()));
                consumer.commitSync();
            }
        }
    }

    private static void processTransfer(String value) {
        // Slow processing is OK -- just means fewer polls per second
    }
}
```

### How does backpressure affect lag?

When a consumer is slower than production, its pull rate falls below the produce rate and lag grows. Lag is the visible, durable record of backpressure.

```java
package com.bank.backpressure;

public class LagAsBackpressure {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int produceRate = 1000; // records/sec
        int consumeRate = 800;  // records/sec
        int deficit = produceRate - consumeRate;

        System.out.println("Produce rate : " + produceRate + " records/sec");
        System.out.println("Consume rate : " + consumeRate + " records/sec");
        System.out.println("Lag growth   : " + deficit + " records/sec");
        System.out.println("After 1 min  : " + (deficit * 60) + " records behind");
        System.out.println("After 1 hour : " + (deficit * 3600) + " records behind");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("All records safe in the log -- nothing lost");
    }
}
```

```text
Produce rate : 1000 records/sec
Consume rate : 800 records/sec
Lag growth   : 200 records/sec
After 1 min  : 12000 records behind
After 1 hour : 720000 records behind

All records safe in the log -- nothing lost
```

### How do you tune poll size safely?

`max.poll.records` bounds the batch, and `max.poll.interval.ms` is the deadline. If processing a batch overruns the interval, the broker thinks the consumer is dead and triggers a rebalance.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class PollSizeTuning {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: huge batch, slow processing
        System.out.println("WRONG: max.poll.records=10000, processing=500ms each");
        System.out.println("  Batch time: 10000 * 500ms = 5000 seconds");
        System.out.println("  max.poll.interval.ms=300000 (5 min)");
        System.out.println("  Result: REBALANCE triggered!");
        System.out.println();

        // CORRECT: smaller batch that fits within poll interval
        System.out.println("CORRECT: max.poll.records=100, processing=500ms each");
        System.out.println("  Batch time: 100 * 500ms = 50 seconds");
        System.out.println("  Well within 5 min poll interval");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: max.poll.records=10000, processing=500ms each
  Batch time: 10000 * 500ms = 5000 seconds
  max.poll.interval.ms=300000 (5 min)
  Result: REBALANCE triggered!

CORRECT: max.poll.records=100, processing=500ms each
  Batch time: 100 * 500ms = 50 seconds
  Well within 5 min poll interval
```

### Why use manual offset commit?

Manual commit keeps lag honest. With auto-commit, offsets advance before processing finishes, which risks data loss on a crash and makes lag understate the true backlog.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class ManualVsAutoCommit {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Auto-commit (enable.auto.commit=true):");
        System.out.println("  Offset committed on a timer, before processing done");
        System.out.println("  Crash -> records lost (committed but not processed)");
        System.out.println("  Lag understates true backlog");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Manual commit (enable.auto.commit=false):");
        System.out.println("  Offset committed only after processing succeeds");
        System.out.println("  Crash -> resume from last truly completed record");
        System.out.println("  Lag honestly reflects outstanding work");
    }
}
```

```text
Auto-commit (enable.auto.commit=true):
  Offset committed on a timer, before processing done
  Crash -> records lost (committed but not processed)
  Lag understates true backlog

Manual commit (enable.auto.commit=false):
  Offset committed only after processing succeeds
  Crash -> resume from last truly completed record
  Lag honestly reflects outstanding work
```

### How do you scale throughput?

Throughput for a consumer group is limited by partition count. Each partition is consumed by at most one consumer in a group.

```java
package com.bank.scaling;

public class ConsumerScaling {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int partitions = 6;
        for (int consumers = 1; consumers <= 8; consumers++) {
            int active = Math.min(consumers, partitions);
            int idle = consumers - active;
            System.out.printf("  %d consumers: %d active, %d idle%n",
                consumers, active, idle);
        }
        System.out.println("  Beyond " + partitions + " consumers, add partitions!");
    }
}
```

```text
  1 consumers: 1 active, 0 idle
  2 consumers: 2 active, 0 idle
  3 consumers: 3 active, 0 idle
  4 consumers: 4 active, 0 idle
  5 consumers: 5 active, 0 idle
  6 consumers: 6 active, 0 idle
  7 consumers: 6 active, 1 idle
  8 consumers: 6 active, 2 idle
  Beyond 6 consumers, add partitions!
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.mistake;

public class BackpressureMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: huge poll batch that overruns poll interval");
        System.out.println("  Consumer kicked out, rebalance makes things worse");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: small batches that finish within poll interval");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: 8 consumers for 6 partitions");
        System.out.println("  2 consumers sit idle -- wasted resources");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: consumers <= partitions, add partitions first");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: huge poll batch that overruns poll interval
  Consumer kicked out, rebalance makes things worse

CORRECT: small batches that finish within poll interval

WRONG: 8 consumers for 6 partitions
  2 consumers sit idle -- wasted resources

CORRECT: consumers <= partitions, add partitions first
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Kafka converts overload into durable lag instead of failure, which buys time to react. Partition count is a capacity-planning decision made early because scaling consumers only helps up to that number. The pull model is why Kafka stays stable under bursty load compared to push-based queues.
